  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=x\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x\\sqrt{2}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x\\sqrt{2}})}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
